{%- comment -%}
  The size chart trigger and panel for the product page.
  In: product (required), section_id (required)
  Renders nothing when the product resolves to no chart.

  Opening the trigger shows the chart itself: the height x weight grid with the
  finder offered below it, or — for a chart with no grid, such as shoe sizes —
  the measurements table alone. The chart is what was asked for; the finder is
  for people who would rather answer two questions.

  Resolution is inline rather than in its own snippet: Shopify forbids
  {% include %} inside a {% render %}ed snippet, and {% render %} cannot
  hand an object back to its caller. The panel is the only consumer, so
  there is nothing to share.

  Order: the product's own override, then the first matching rule by
  position, then nothing. There is deliberately no fallback chart —
  showing a chart that may not describe the item is worse than none.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign sc_chart = nil -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Which chart a product gets.

  Order: the product's own override, then the first matching rule by position,
  then nothing. There is deliberately no fallback chart — showing one that may
  not describe the item is worse than showing none.

  A rule is a set of optional conditions, and it matches only when EVERY
  condition it sets matches. Conditions left empty are ignored. That is what
  lets one brand carry two charts: "Nike + tag Men's Sneakers" at position 10
  wins over plain "Nike" at position 90, because brand alone is too coarse —
  Nike here is 54 footwear and 5 clothing.

  match_type/match_value are the original single-condition form and are still
  honoured, so rules written before this change behave exactly as they did.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if product.metafields.custom.size_chart_override.value != blank -%}
  {%- assign sc_chart = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_override.value -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign sc_rules = shop.metaobjects.size_chart_rule.values | sort: 'position' -%}
  {%- assign sc_vendor = product.vendor | downcase | strip -%}
  {%- assign sc_type = product.type | downcase | strip -%}

  {%- for rule in sc_rules -%}
    {%- assign matched = true -%}
    {%- assign has_condition = false -%}

    {%- if rule.brand != blank -%}
      {%- assign has_condition = true -%}
      {%- assign want = rule.brand | downcase | strip -%}
      {%- unless want == sc_vendor -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if rule.product_type != blank -%}
      {%- assign has_condition = true -%}
      {%- assign want = rule.product_type | downcase | strip -%}
      {%- unless want == sc_type -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if rule.tag != blank -%}
      {%- assign has_condition = true -%}
      {%- assign want = rule.tag | downcase | strip -%}
      {%- assign tag_hit = false -%}
      {%- for t in product.tags -%}
        {%- assign have = t | downcase | strip -%}
        {%- if have == want -%}{%- assign tag_hit = true -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- unless tag_hit -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Title keywords. This is the only thing that separates an Essentials hoodie
      from an Essentials tee: all of them carry the garment in the title, while
      product type is "Clothing" for every one of them and no tag says which.

      Several words separated by commas must ALL appear: "hoodie, ss22". The
      season is what picks between Essentials charts — there is one hoodie
      chart per season — so the garment alone is not enough. A single word
      behaves exactly as it did before.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if rule.title_contains != blank -%}
      {%- assign has_condition = true -%}
      {%- assign sc_title = product.title | downcase -%}
      {%- assign words = rule.title_contains | downcase | split: ',' -%}
      {%- for word in words -%}
        {%- assign want = word | strip -%}
        {%- if want != blank -%}
          {%- unless sc_title contains want -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} The original single-condition form. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if rule.match_type != blank and rule.match_value != blank -%}
      {%- assign has_condition = true -%}
      {%- assign want = rule.match_value | downcase | strip -%}
      {%- if rule.match_type == 'brand' -%}
        {%- unless want == sc_vendor -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
      {%- elsif rule.match_type == 'product_type' -%}
        {%- unless want == sc_type -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
      {%- elsif rule.match_type == 'tag' -%}
        {%- assign tag_hit = false -%}
        {%- for t in product.tags -%}
          {%- assign have = t | downcase | strip -%}
          {%- if have == want -%}{%- assign tag_hit = true -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- unless tag_hit -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
      {%- elsif rule.match_type == 'title_contains' -%}
        {%- assign sc_title = product.title | downcase -%}
        {%- unless sc_title contains want -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
      {%- elsif rule.match_type == 'collection' -%}
        {%- assign coll_hit = false -%}
        {%- for coll in product.collections -%}
          {%- if coll.handle == want -%}{%- assign coll_hit = true -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- unless coll_hit -%}{%- assign matched = false -%}{%- endunless -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- assign matched = false -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A rule with no conditions at all would match everything, which is how a
      half-finished rule silently puts the wrong chart on the whole catalogue.
      It matches nothing instead.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if has_condition and matched and rule.chart.value != blank -%}
      {%- assign sc_chart = rule.chart.value -%}
      {%- break -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if sc_chart -%}
  {%- assign panel_id = 'size-chart-' | append: section_id | append: '-' | append: product.id -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Two callers, two shells.

    Embedded, the panel is a view inside the size sheet, so it emits its body
    alone — the sheet already supplies the overlay, the bar and the close
    button. A second set of them is what made the old arrangement fight itself:
    the guide button had to close one popover and open another on a timer, and
    anything that slowed the close left the guide opening into nothing.

    Standalone, it emits only the trigger link that sits beside the SIZE label.
    The script points that at the sheet too, so there is one chart on the page
    and one way in.
  {%- endcomment -%}

  {%- if embedded -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The host wrapper belongs here, not in the caller: rendered there it
      appeared even on products that resolve to no chart, and the script that
      reveals the "Size guide" button only checks that a host exists — so the
      button offered an empty panel.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="size-chart__host" data-size-chart-host hidden>
    <div class="popover__content size-chart__panel" data-size-chart-views>
      <div data-size-chart-view="chart">
        {%- if sc_chart.grid != blank -%}
          <section class="size-chart__section">
            <h2 class="size-chart__heading">Height &amp; weight</h2>
            {%- render 'size-chart-grid', raw: sc_chart.grid, caption: sc_chart.grid_note -%}
          </section>

          <button type="button" class="size-chart__nav" data-size-chart-to="finder">
            <span>Not sure? Find my size</span>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
          </button>
        {%- elsif sc_chart.measurements != blank -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            A chart with no height x weight grid — shoe conversions, garment
            measurements — is a plain table, so it is shown as one. There is
            nothing for the finder to look up in it, so it offers none. It goes
            through the grid snippet for the scroller: shoe tables run six
            columns, too wide for a phone.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <section class="size-chart__section">
            <h2 class="size-chart__heading">Size chart</h2>
            {%- render 'size-chart-grid', raw: sc_chart.measurements, caption: sc_chart.measurements_note -%}
          </section>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- if sc_chart.grid != blank -%}
        <div data-size-chart-view="finder" hidden>
          <button type="button" class="size-chart__nav size-chart__nav--back" data-size-chart-to="chart">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7"/></svg>
            <span>Back to the chart</span>
          </button>

          <section class="size-chart__section">
            <h2 class="size-chart__heading">Find my size</h2>
            {%- render 'size-chart-finder', raw: sc_chart.grid, measurements: sc_chart.measurements, row_label: sc_chart.row_label, col_label: sc_chart.col_label, id_suffix: panel_id -%}
          </section>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
    </div>
  {%- else -%}
    <button
      type="button"
      class="product-form__option-link size-chart__trigger link text--subdued"
      data-open-size-chart-link
    >{{ 'product.general.size_chart' | t }}</button>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
